[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage now. Both gateway SVIs for the two Campus 2 floors are up and reachable:
- From pc1 (10.5.0.1) to 10.5.0.254: 0% loss, sub‑ms RTT.
- From pc3 (10.6.0.3) to 10.6.0.254: 0% loss, sub‑ms RTT.

On the device hosting the gateways (sshswitch), the L3 interfaces are present and active:
- br_v5 has 10.5.0.254/24, br_v6 has 10.6.0.254/24 (ip addr).
- The VLAN access to these bridges is forwarding: eth0.5 and eth0.6 are in br_v5/br_v6, and are in state “forwarding” (bridge link).
On the access switch for those floors (switch4), the VLAN plumbing is up:
- eth0.5 master br_vlan5 and eth0.6 master br_vlan6 are UP with member access ports for the respective user ports (ip addr/ifconfig).

Mechanism: Both floors share the same L2 trunk (ms1_sw4) to sshswitch and rely on sshswitch’s SVIs. A reload could transiently remove or delay bringing up VLAN subinterfaces/bridges, simultaneously breaking ARP/ICMP to both 10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254. Localization: sshswitch (br_v5/br_v6, eth0.5/eth0.6) and the trunk to switch4. Root cause: Not reproducible now; most consistent with non-persistent or delayed post-reload VLAN/SVI bring-up on sshswitch.

Corrective fix:
- Ensure sshswitch boot config persists and autostarts: define br_v5/br_v6 and eth0.{5,6} (and bond0.{5,6} if used) in the system’s startup (e.g., /etc/network/interfaces or netplan) and enable the network service.
- Save/commit configuration changes so they survive reloads.
- Add a post-boot health check to verify br_v5/br_v6 are up and respond to pings from a floor host.
=== END ===